content:
 After entering society, I&rsquo;ve really encountered all kinds of troubles.
Just to give some examples: using criminal threats for civil matters (thankfully not prosecuted in the end), cars parked blocking entrances, bringing pets to pee at doorsteps, treating others&rsquo; balconies as trash bins&hellip; etc.
I used to care a lot about these things, but over the past year or two, I&rsquo;ve gradually become more composed about them.
Recently, I read &ldquo;The Almanack of Naval Ravikant: A Guide to Wealth and Happiness&rdquo; and came across a passage that deeply resonated with me:
&ldquo;You always have three options: change it, accept it, or leave it. The worst thing is to do nothing, hoping for change without taking action, knowing you can&rsquo;t leave but unable to accept. Most of life&rsquo;s suffering comes from this struggle, or from weariness with reality. The phrase I say to myself most often is: &lsquo;Just accept it.&rsquo;&rdquo;
&ldquo;What I mean by acceptance is being able to remain calm no matter the outcome, finding balance and center. Often, taking a step back gives you a clearer view of the whole picture. No one can always get what they want. Accept what has happened with equanimity—the sooner you accept reality, the faster you can adapt. Of course, accepting reality isn&rsquo;t always easy.&rdquo;
Many times, reality is harsh—&ldquo;changing&rdquo; others is too difficult, and the cost of &ldquo;leaving&rdquo; the environment is too high. After the process of elimination, we&rsquo;re often left with only one option: &ldquo;acceptance.&rdquo;
But acceptance doesn&rsquo;t mean surrender. Since objective facts can&rsquo;t be changed, at least my subjective state of mind is mine to control. Since I have to accept it anyway, instead of being bitter and resentful, why not try to face it with a more open, even slightly humorous mindset?
After all, life isn&rsquo;t about pursuing fairness and justice, but about seeking inner peace.
